// The Halcyon Launch, v2 DRAFT. Chapter 2, "Unexplained, Not Repeatable." Not loaded by index.html or courses.js. Drop-in shape for full/ch02.js.
// Written 2026-10-11 to full/BIBLE_v2_proposal.md (chapter 2 plan, flags table, Decisions 2026-10-11) and in the format of full/v2/ch01.js.
// Sources read for this chapter (verified 2026-10-11):
// PMI, PMP Examination Content Outline, July 2026 PMP Certification Exam Update (PDF, read in full):
//   https://www.pmi.org/-/media/pmi/documents/public/pdf/certifications/new-pmp-examination-content-outline-2026.pdf
//   Enablers used here, by the course's codes: R1 (create an integrated project management plan; assess consolidated project plans for
//   dependencies, gaps, and continued business value; maintain the integrated plan; determine critical information requirements; collect and
//   analyze data), R2 (define scope; obtain stakeholder agreement on project scope; break down scope), R8 (prepare a schedule based on the selected
//   development approach; coordinate with other projects and operations; create and baseline a project schedule), B2 (confirm compliance
//   requirements, e.g. security, health and safety, sustainability, regulatory; classify compliance categories; determine potential threats to
//   compliance; analyze the consequences of noncompliance; measure the extent to which the project is in compliance), B3 (execute the change control
//   process; communicate the status of proposed changes; implement approved changes; update project documentation), B5 (identify, analyze, monitor
//   and control risks; maintain a risk register; communicate the status of a risk impact), P4 (build trust and influence stakeholders; analyze and
//   tailor communication), P6 (identify internal and external customer expectations; align and maintain outcomes to them; monitor satisfaction).
// PMI Code of Ethics and Professional Conduct (PDF): https://www.pmi.org/-/media/pmi/documents/public/pdf/ethics/pmi-code-of-ethics.pdf
//   2.2.5 (errors or omissions by ourselves or others: prompt corrective action, tell the appropriate authority), 2.3.1 (inform ourselves of and
//   uphold the policies, rules, regulations, and laws that govern our work), 2.3.5 (no retaliation for good faith ethical concerns), 3.2.3 (actively
//   listen to others' points of view), 5.2.1 (earnestly seek to understand the truth), 5.2.5 (an environment where others feel safe to tell the
//   truth), 5.3.1 (no half-truths, information out of context, or misleading omissions).
// PMBOK Guide 8th ed. (PMI, 2025), checked against the book 2026-10-11 (Std = The Standard for Project Management, bound in the same volume):
//   the principle Adopt a Holistic View, systems thinking about components and their interdependencies (Std 3.3, p. 38); seven performance
//   domains, Governance, Scope (including quality), Schedule, Finance, Stakeholders, Resources, Risk, with 40 nonprescriptive processes (Guide
//   Sec. 2, pp. 7 to 8). The chapter claims no order for the domains. WBS, backlog, and integrated plan teaching follows the ECO and standard PMI
//   terms, not PMBOK 8 wording.
// Light time: JPL Horizons, Mars (499) observed from Earth center (500@399), quantity 20, read 2026-10-11:
//   https://ssd.jpl.nasa.gov/api/horizons.api?format=text&COMMAND='499'&CENTER='500@399'&MAKE_EPHEM='YES'&EPHEM_TYPE='OBSERVER'&START_TIME='2061-02-05'&STOP_TIME='2061-04-01'&STEP_SIZE='1%20d'&QUANTITIES='20'
//   Feb 28 2061 00:00 UT 0.78053 AU, Mar 1 0.77349 AU; at 12:40 UT (5:40 AM MST) about 0.77681 AU = one way 387.6 s = 6 min 28 s.
//   Apr 1 2061 0.6365 AU; the minimum (about 0.63 AU, 5.3 min) is Apr 8 per the proposal, so "a little over five" minutes in April is right.
// Sol: SOL 3518 on Jan 10 2061 (ch1) plus 49 days / 1.02749 = 47.7 sols, so Ada records SOL 3565 the evening before Sam hears it Feb 28.
// Weekdays checked: Fri Feb 4, Mon Feb 7, Tue Feb 8, Thu Feb 10, Mon Feb 14, Wed Feb 16, Fri Feb 18, Tue Feb 22, Thu Feb 24, Mon Feb 28, Thu Mar 3,
//   Wed Mar 9, Mon Mar 21, Fri Mar 25 2061; Tue May 23 2034 (Petrel L minus 385 to Jun 12 2035); Mon Aug 18 2059. Countdown to Feb 18 2063:
//   744 on Feb 4, 741 Feb 7, 738 Feb 10, 730 Feb 18 (two years to the day), 711 Mar 9.
// MARGIN TABLE (continues ch1's). Margin = days of breathable reserve at the current stack decline, as of the date Sam hears the log.
//   Jan 10 2061 (ch1): Ada 952 (runs out Aug 20 2063); ARA model and Hollis 1,012 (Oct 19 2063). Arrival on plan Sep 6 2063.
//   Feb 28 2061 (ch2): 49 days elapsed. The station's first ration cut (calories down a tenth, gym sessions halved, lights out earlier): Ada credits
//   it with 8 days, Hollis's report with 15. Ada 952 minus 49 plus 8 = 911 (runs out Aug 28 2063). ARA/Hollis 1,012 minus 49 plus 15 = 978 (Nov 3 2063).
//   Arrival is day 920 from Feb 28. On Ada's number the ship arrives 9 days after the reserve ends ("minus nine"); on the ARA's, 58 to spare.
//   The gap between the two numbers is now 67 days ("about two months"). No per person oxygen figure is used.
// Mass line in f2s10 (each kilo on the habitat costs about two of propellant) is a rough Tsiolkovsky check: about 4 km/s from low Earth orbit for a
//   fast 200 day transfer at an Isp near 370 s gives a mass ratio near 3, so roughly 2 kg of propellant per kg of payload. Fictional program figure.
// Invented for the story: every person, company, agency, document, number, rule, and event. Flight Rule 2.36, the ARA criticality scale, Question
//   61-04, Aldergrove P/N 7740-112, and the Petrel are fictional.
(window.HALCYON_FULL=window.HALCYON_FULL||[]).push({
  num: 2, part: `Part One: The Window`, title: `Unexplained, Not Repeatable`, weeks: `February 4 to March 25, 2061`,
  tasks: [`R1`,`P4`,`B5`,`R2`,`B2`,`P6`,`B3`,`R8`],
  cast: [
    [`Wendell Price`,`Test conductor, 69. Ran the stand the day the valve twitched. Answers exactly the question you ask him.`],
    [`Hana Iwasaki`,`Propulsion lead. Owns the departure stage, and the closed report on its main valve.`],
    [`Quinn Abernathy`,`Sam's deputy. Puts both hands flat on the table when it's bad, and is good at finding out when it is.`],
    [`Rafa Quintero`,`Flight software lead. His scope is a backlog, and he'd like it to stay one.`],
    [`Garrett Lyle`,`Tallgrass CEO. Wants the plan to show float, because investors can read float.`],
    [`Imogen Hale`,`ARA Director and the program's sponsor. Would like forty million people to watch the ship leave.`],
    [`Yusra Haddad`,`Halcyon's commander, 44. Four kids in Aurora. Signed up for a one way flight in 2059.`],
    [`Frances Odum`,`Chair of Families of Ares. Her son Malik is on the station. Doesn't say hello on the phone.`],
    [`Ada Okafor`,`Sam's daughter, on Ares Station. Has tomatoes, and a question about a valve.`],
    [`Ruth Calder`,`On the Independent Readiness Board. Still not his mentor. Sends pages in the mail.`]
  ],
  opening: [
    `Friday, February 4, 2061. 5:25 AM. Sam hadn't slept. Around three he'd stopped pretending and printed AR 2207 on the printer in Ada's room, the one with a cartoon tomato sticker on the lid that she'd put there at eleven, and at a quarter to five he'd put his boots on and driven east on I-70 in the dark with the printout on the passenger seat, face up, as if it wanted to see where they were going.`,
    `The test stand was two miles past the high bay on a gravel road the county had stopped plowing in 2050. In the dark it was a tower of white lights standing in the middle of nothing, with the departure stage bolted into it, frost blankets over its lower half like somebody had tucked it in. The wind came across the prairie with nothing to stop it between here and Kansas and leaned on the truck hard enough that he felt it in the wheel. Wendell Price's pickup was already in the lot. It was always already in the lot.`,
    `Wendell was in the blockhouse with his boots up on a cable tray and the lid of a thermos balanced on his knee, watching four screens that showed the stage from four sides. He didn't turn around. <span class="said" data-who="Wendell">"You found Bea's folder."</span>`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Sam">"How'd you know?"</span>`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Wendell">"Nobody drives out here at five in the morning to look at a building."</span> He took his boots off the tray. <span class="said" data-who="Wendell">"She was going to ask me about it. December. She had it on a sticky note."</span>`,
    `Sam thought of the note on the bezel of Bea's monitor, square capitals. ASK W ABOUT STAND. He'd read it every morning for four weeks and taken it for a reminder about a seal.`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Sam">"Did she ask you?"</span>`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Wendell">"No. She ran out of December."</span> He pointed at the third screen, at a gray cylinder about the size of a beer keg low on the stage, in a nest of lines, wrapped in its own blanket. <span class="said" data-who="Wendell">"That's her. Main oxidizer isolation valve. August of '59, she twitched on cycle three. Forty milliseconds. We cycled her twice more that afternoon and she was perfect both times."</span>`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Sam">"The report says stand noise."</span>`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Wendell">"Stand had noise that summer. Ground fault on the instrumentation bus, June till we found it in September. Three channels spiking. I wrote it up."</span>`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Sam">"So it was noise."</span>`,
    `Wendell looked at him for the first time. <span class="said" data-who="Wendell">"I said the stand had noise. Nobody asked me if that valve did. They asked if the stand had noise that summer, and I said yes, because it did."</span> He picked up the thermos lid, found it empty, and set it down again. <span class="said" data-who="Wendell">"Pull the file. Look at the pressure channel downstream of her. Then come back out here and ask me the right question."</span>`,
    `By 8:30 the file was on the big screen in the program room, and Quinn had it plotted. She'd come in at seven without being asked, because she'd seen his truck in the lot at six, which he'd forgotten she would.`,
    `<prop type="doc" title="STAND 2 DATA · MOIV-1 ACCEPTANCE · MON AUG 18 2059 · CYCLE 3 OF 5">T+0 MS: CLOSE COMMAND.<br>T+208 MS: LVDT POSITION AT CLOSED. LIMIT SWITCH CLOSED.<br>T+214 TO T+254 MS: LVDT REPORTS POPPET 0.4 MM OFF SEAT. LIMIT SWITCH CLOSED. (DISAGREEMENT 40 MS.)<br>T+254 MS: LVDT AT CLOSED. AGREEMENT RESTORED.<br>CH 31, DOWNSTREAM PRESSURE (RACK B): RIPPLE, 3.1 PSI PEAK, T+216 TO T+251 MS.<br>NOTE: CH 31 ON RACK B. LVDT ON RACK A. BOTH RACKS ON INSTRUMENTATION GROUND BUS 2.</prop>`,
    `Quinn put her finger on the screen, on the little hump in channel 31. <span class="said" data-who="Quinn">"That's on a different rack. If it's noise, it's noise that happened on two racks at once and knew exactly when to start."</span> She read the last line again. <span class="said" data-who="Quinn">"Same ground bus, though."</span> She sat back. <span class="said" data-who="Quinn">"So it's the valve coming off its seat and letting a little oxygen past, or it's the ground fault, and this file can't tell you which. Two retests, same afternoon, same stand, same crew, same ground fault."</span> She had both hands flat on the table.`,
    `At 8:55 Hana Iwasaki came in early for the nine o'clock with her laptop under her arm, saw the plot on the wall, and stopped a step inside the door.`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Hana">"That's closed,"</span> she said. She said it to the plot. She didn't look at Sam. She set her laptop down on the table, picked it back up, said she'd be at the stand, and left, and the door took its time closing behind her on the pneumatic arm.`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Quinn">"She was in the room when it closed,"</span> Quinn said quietly. <span class="said" data-who="Quinn">"Everybody was."</span>`,
    `Sam looked at the forty milliseconds for a long time after Quinn went to get coffee. He knew what this was. A form somebody had closed with a doubt still behind it. He knew how that felt from the inside, because once, in Arizona, in 2041, he'd been the one holding the pen, with an indication on a jet that came and went and an hour he didn't have. He didn't think about that, as a rule. The rule had held for nineteen years. It wasn't holding this morning.`,
    `At 9:14 Garrett's email came in. <b>Sam. Readiness Board wants the integrated plan for its March 21 session. All of it. Let's talk Monday. G.</b> Over the high bay doors the countdown clock said 744. Sam folded the printout of AR 2207 in half and put it in his desk drawer. Then he took it back out and laid it flat on top of the desk, where he'd have to look at it.`
  ],
  lesson: {
    title: `Ruth's Console Log, page two: the gate and the bars`,
    sections: [
      { h: `Page two`,
        body: [`<prop type="doc" kind="consolelog" title="PETREL FLOW · L MINUS 385 DAYS · MAY 23 2034">0630 INTEGRATED TIMELINE UP ON THE WALL, FLOW 3. ELEVEN SHEETS, TAPED END TO END. WENDELL SAYS THE TAPE WILL OUTLAST THE PROGRAM.<br>0700 EVERY SUBSYSTEM DREW ITS OWN BARS. PROP. GNC. HAB. CREW. RANGE. NOBODY'S BARS TOUCH ANYBODY ELSE'S. THAT'S A LIE. WENT DOWN THE WALL WITH THE RED MARKER.<br>0840 41 PLACES WHERE THE BARS TOUCH. NOBODY OWNS ANY OF THEM. I DO NOW.<br>0915 DREW THE WINDOW AT THE RIGHT EDGE. TWO LINES. MORI SAYS IT LOOKS LIKE A GATE.<br>0916 IT IS A GATE.<br>0920 ACROSS THE TOP: THE GATE IS REAL. THE BARS IN FRONT OF IT ARE GUESSES.<br>1100 ADMINISTRATOR WALKED THROUGH. ASKED WHY THE BARS HAVE FUZZY ENDS. TOLD HIM BECAUSE THEY DO.<br>1400 SCOPE REVIEW. WHAT PETREL WON'T CARRY. TATE ASKED FOR HER KEYBOARD. 4 KG. DENIED. SHE SAID SHE'LL HUM.<br>1730 L MINUS 385. TAPE HOLDING.</prop>`,
          `That's me in May of 2034, five weeks into the job. I was proud of the red marker. I'd never seen anyone go down a wall of other people's schedules circling the places where they touched, and I thought I'd invented something. I hadn't. I'd found out what an integrated plan is for.`,
          `The ECO's first Process task is develop an integrated project management plan and plan delivery. You met the front half of its enablers on page one: assess the project's needs and complexity, recommend an approach. The rest are about the plan itself. Create it. Estimate the effort and the resources. Assess the consolidated plans for dependencies, gaps, and continued business value. Maintain it. Collect and analyze data so the decisions it drives are informed ones.`,
          `The integrated plan is the document that says how the project will be done, watched, and closed. It pulls together the subsidiary plans for scope, schedule, cost, quality, resources, communications, risk, procurement, and stakeholder engagement, plus the baselines the work gets measured against. Most of those plans have owners other than you. The leads should write their own parts, because a plan is how people agree to work together, and nobody agrees to a plan somebody else wrote for them. You own the whole thing, and you own the places where the parts touch. Each of my eleven sheets was a good plan. The forty one red circles were where the program would actually come apart, because nobody had to answer for them.`,
          `The PMBOK Guide's eighth edition names a holistic view among its principles. That's this wall. In a hybrid project the agile team's release plan and cadence belong inside the integrated plan, with the dates where its work meets the hardware fixed and owned. A board covered in sticky notes is a perfectly good plan for software. It's one part of a plan for a ship.`,
          `And the gate. Write my 0920 line next to whatever you're planning. The window is a constraint, about as hard as constraints get. Every bar in front of it is an estimate somebody made on a particular Tuesday. A plan that treats its estimates as if they were the gate will break the first time one of them turns out to be a guess. Every one of them is.`],
        terms: [[`Integrated project management plan`,`The document that describes how the project will be executed, monitored and controlled, and closed. It joins the subsidiary plans and the baselines.`],[`Subsidiary plans`,`The component plans: scope, schedule, cost, quality, resources, communications, risk, procurement, stakeholder engagement, and others the project needs.`],[`Baselines`,`The approved scope, schedule, and cost that performance is measured against; together, the performance measurement baseline.`],[`Constraint`,`A limiting factor the project has to work within, such as a launch window nobody can move.`]],
        exam: `If a question asks who owns the integrated plan, it's the project manager, built with the team. If it asks what to update after an approved change, think of the plan and its baselines, not just the schedule.` },
      { h: `What won't fly`,
        body: [`At 1400 that day we held the scope review, and Roz Tate asked to bring her keyboard. An electric piano, four kilos with the stand. She played it in the evenings in the crew quarters at the Cape, the same six songs, not well, and Lise said she'd fly the ship to Mars by hand if it meant never hearing the fourth one again. We said no. Mass. It's on page two in my handwriting, and I've read that line more times than anything else on the page.`,
          `The ECO's R2 is develop and manage project scope, and its enablers are short: define scope, obtain stakeholder agreement on project scope, break down scope. You define it in the project scope statement. It describes the product and the project's deliverables, the acceptance criteria for each, the assumptions you're making, the constraints you're under, and what's excluded.`,
          `Exclusions are the part people skip, and they're the part that matters most later. Every stakeholder carries a picture of what the project will deliver. Wherever that picture runs past what you're building, you have an argument coming. Writing the exclusion down with its reason, in words a family member could read, is how you have that argument early, while it's cheap. Leave it for launch day and it costs you everybody's trust at once.`,
          `Then get the agreement. Defining scope is the project's job. Agreeing to it belongs to the stakeholders it touches, and for the hard exclusions that means sitting down with the people they hurt before the document reaches them.`,
          `Two words the exam likes. Scope creep is uncontrolled growth in scope, usually one small, reasonable request at a time. Gold plating is the team adding things nobody asked for, because it could. Both skip the agreement step, and both cost mass, whatever your project's version of mass happens to be.`],
        terms: [[`Project scope statement`,`Describes the product, deliverables, acceptance criteria, assumptions, constraints, and exclusions.`],[`Exclusions`,`What the project will not deliver, stated explicitly so expectations match the scope.`],[`Scope creep`,`Uncontrolled expansion of scope without adjusting time, cost, and resources.`],[`Gold plating`,`Adding features or extras the customer didn't ask for.`]],
        exam: `When stakeholders assume something is included that isn't, clarify the scope with them and document the exclusion. Quietly adding it is scope creep, and promising to study it to keep them happy sets up a bigger gap.` },
      { h: `Boxes and a list`,
        body: [`Now break it down. A work breakdown structure is a hierarchy that decomposes the total scope of the project, deliverable by deliverable, until you reach work packages: pieces small enough to estimate, schedule, cost, and hand to one owner. The WBS dictionary describes each element, what it is, what done looks like, who owns it, what it depends on. The approved scope statement, the WBS, and the WBS dictionary together are the scope baseline.`,
          `Two rules carry most of the weight. Decompose by deliverable. A WBS organized by department tells you who sits where and hides everything that falls between the desks. Then check the 100 percent rule: the children of any element add up to all of the parent's work and nothing outside it. Check that honestly and you find the work nobody owned. There's always some.`,
          `Then the backlog. A product backlog is an ordered list of everything the team might build for the product, ranked by value and refined continuously by the product owner. The items near the top are small, clear, and ready. The ones near the bottom are coarse, and they're supposed to be. It changes every sprint, and that's what it's for.`,
          `In a hybrid project you don't have to pick one. The WBS holds the whole scope, and one element of it, the software, gets delivered adaptively. That element's content is the backlog. Its releases are tied to integration dates in the schedule. Anything in the backlog that needs hardware names the WBS element it touches, so a new feature that needs a new sensor turns up as a hardware question while there's still time to buy the sensor. Planning near work in detail and far work in outline, and adding detail as you learn, is rolling wave planning, and it works on both sides of the seam.`],
        terms: [[`Work breakdown structure (WBS)`,`A deliverable-oriented hierarchical decomposition of the total scope of work.`],[`Work package`,`The lowest level of the WBS, where work can be estimated, scheduled, costed, and owned.`],[`100 percent rule`,`Each level of the WBS contains all the work of the level above it, and nothing more.`],[`WBS dictionary`,`Detail for each WBS element: description, acceptance criteria, owner, dependencies.`],[`Scope baseline`,`The approved scope statement, WBS, and WBS dictionary.`],[`Product backlog`,`An ordered list of work for the product, ranked by value and refined continuously by the product owner.`],[`Rolling wave planning`,`Planning near-term work in detail and later work at a higher level, adding detail as it becomes known.`]],
        exam: `Work discovered late, owned by nobody, usually points to the 100 percent rule. In hybrid scenarios, pick the answer that keeps one scope, with the adaptive part delivered from a backlog and joined to the plan at defined milestones.` },
      { h: `The baseline, and changing it`,
        body: [`R8, plan and manage schedule, has enablers worth reading slowly: prepare a schedule based on the selected development approach, coordinate with other projects and operations, estimate, use benchmarks and history, create the schedule, baseline it, run the schedule management plan, analyze variation. Notice the order. You baseline after the estimates have come from the people doing the work, and after you've checked the schedule against everything else that needs the same people and the same facilities. A stand that's down for a seal change is operations, and operations doesn't read your schedule.`,
          `A baseline is the approved version of the schedule, and later of the scope and the cost, that progress gets measured against. It's approved through governance, usually by the sponsor, and it changes only through change control. In a hybrid project you baseline hardware in detail and software at the release level, and leave the backlog free to move underneath. Baselining every user story turns ordinary backlog refinement into a weekly flood of variances that mean nothing.`,
          `Which brings me to change control, the ECO's B3: execute the change control process, communicate the status of proposed changes, implement approved changes, update the documentation. The process is plain. Someone asks for a change. It gets written down as a change request. The people who'd do the work analyze its impact on scope, schedule, cost, quality, risk, and people. A change control board, or whoever your governance names, decides at the right level. The plan and its baselines get updated. The person who asked hears where it stands, including when the answer is no.`,
          `Every change goes through that, whoever asks. A sponsor's request is a change request with an important name on it. On an agile team, reordering the backlog inside a release is the product owner's ordinary work and doesn't need a board. When a change crosses the baseline, or the seam between the software and the hardware, it goes through change control, however small and quick the team that would build it.`],
        terms: [[`Schedule baseline`,`The approved schedule used as the basis for comparison; changed only through formal change control.`],[`Change request`,`A formal proposal to modify a document, deliverable, or baseline.`],[`Change control board (CCB)`,`The group responsible for reviewing, evaluating, and deciding on change requests.`],[`Impact analysis`,`Assessing what a proposed change would do to scope, schedule, cost, quality, risk, and resources.`]],
        exam: `The first move with a change request is usually to analyze its impact. Implementing it before approval, or turning it down on your own, are the traps, even when the request comes from the sponsor.` },
      { h: `A list of how people die`,
        body: [`My register in 2034 had six hundred lines and not one of them had a person in it. We wrote in codes. Prop margin. Thermal concern. We knew what the codes meant. Codes let you read six hundred lines a week without feeling any of them, and I think that was the point of them, and I think it was a mistake.`,
          `B5 is plan and manage risk: identify risks, analyze them, develop the risk management plan, maintain the register, execute the responses, monitor and control, and communicate the status of each risk's impact. A risk is an uncertain event or condition that would affect the project if it happened. An issue is one that has happened. The register carries each risk's cause, event, and effect, its probability and impact, an owner, a planned response, and the trigger that tells you it's starting.`,
          `Here's what I want you to keep. Probability and impact are separate axes, and you assess them separately. Impact comes from the consequence: what happens, and to whom, if this goes wrong. Probability comes from the evidence. The quickest way to talk yourself out of a risk is to let one leak into the other, to call a failure less serious because it seems unlikely. On flight programs the impact scale often has its own name, criticality, set by the worst credible consequence of the failure mode. Criticality 1 means you could lose the crew or the vehicle. How often you think it'll happen doesn't come into it.`,
          `Write your risks so a tired person can read them. If this happens, then this, and this is who gets hurt. And when you don't know the likelihood, write unknown. A one means you know it's rare. The register is no place to guess politely.`],
        terms: [[`Risk`,`An uncertain event or condition that, if it occurs, affects one or more project objectives.`],[`Issue`,`A current condition or problem affecting the project. A risk that has occurred becomes an issue.`],[`Risk register`,`The record of identified risks with their analysis, owners, responses, triggers, and status.`],[`Risk owner`,`The person accountable for monitoring a risk and carrying out its response.`],[`Criticality (in this book)`,`The ARA's severity scale, set by the worst credible consequence of a failure mode: crit 1 loss of crew or vehicle, crit 2 loss of mission, crit 3 degraded mission. Fictional, modeled on real flight practice.`]],
        exam: `If a scenario lowers a risk's impact because the event seems rare, it's mixing the axes. Impact follows the consequence. Probability follows the evidence.` },
      { h: `Rules, and the ways around them`,
        body: [`B2 is plan and manage project compliance. Confirm the compliance requirements; the ECO's examples are security, health and safety, sustainability, and regulatory compliance. Classify them. Determine the potential threats to compliance. Use methods that support it. Analyze the consequences of noncompliance. Decide what to do. Measure how far the project actually complies.`,
          `A flight rule is a compliance requirement in the safety category, and on a program like Halcyon it binds. Code 2.3.1, a mandatory standard, says we inform ourselves of, and uphold, the policies, rules, regulations, and laws that govern our work. Upholding a rule includes upholding what it's for.`,
          `The threat to compliance you'll meet most often isn't somebody breaking a rule. It's somebody finding its edge. A rule applies above a threshold, so the item gets classified just under the threshold. A review kicks in above a dollar figure, so the order gets split in two. Each instance looks fine on its own. When the same move turns up more than once, near the same kind of deadline, you have a pattern, and the pattern is the finding. Measure it, report it to the governance that owns the rule, and propose a control that closes the edge.`,
          `There's a rule in the ARA's book with my initials on it. I'll tell you about it when we get there. For now I'll only say that the person who writes a rule knows exactly where its edges are, and lies awake about them.`],
        terms: [[`Compliance requirement`,`An obligation from law, regulation, standard, contract, or policy that the project must meet.`],[`Threat to compliance`,`Anything that could make the project fall short of a requirement, including workarounds that meet its letter and defeat its purpose.`],[`Code 2.3.1`,`Inform yourself of, and uphold, the policies, rules, regulations, and laws that govern your work.`]],
        exam: `For compliance problems, the best answer measures the extent of the problem and reports it through governance. Quietly fixing the records, or certifying that the letter of the rule was met, are the distractors.` },
      { h: `Opening a closed thing`,
        body: [`Sooner or later you'll find something closed that shouldn't have been. A report, a decision, a test. Somebody you respect signed it, everyone around you is tired, and opening it will cost weeks.`,
          `The Code is plain about what you owe. 2.2.5: when we discover errors or omissions, by ourselves or others, we take prompt corrective action, and we tell the appropriate authority as soon as we find them. 5.2.1: we earnestly seek to understand the truth. And 5.3.1, from page one, rules out withholding what would make your statements misleading. A closed report you don't believe in is a statement.`,
          `It's just as plain about how. 3.2.3: we actively listen to other people's points of view, seeking to understand them. So you go to the person who made the call first, privately, with the evidence, and you listen. Almost nobody closes a crit 1 report out of malice. They close it because they believed something, or feared something, usually both at once. 5.2.5 asks you to build an environment where people feel safe to tell the truth, and 2.3.5, a mandatory standard, says we don't retaliate against anyone for raising a good faith concern. The person who signed the closure often has the most to tell you once it's open, if you haven't made an enemy of them on the way.`,
          `Then you use the process, formally, where people can see it. A quiet look in a drawer doesn't count. A fresh piece of paper stapled to the old one doesn't either. Ethical failures on projects are nearly always organizational: a tired room, a date, a dozen people who each did one small reasonable thing. The Code still asks each person in that room to speak. On this page, that's you.`],
        terms: [[`Code 2.2.5`,`When we discover errors or omissions, by ourselves or others, we take prompt corrective action and tell the appropriate authority.`],[`Code 3.2.3`,`Actively listen to others' points of view, seeking to understand them.`],[`Code 2.3.5`,`Don't retaliate against people who raise good faith ethical concerns.`]],
        exam: `When a scenario involves a colleague's questionable decision, the best first step is usually a private conversation to understand it, then the formal process. Public confrontation and silence are both distractors.` },
      { h: `What the families expect`,
        body: [`The last task on this page is P6, manage stakeholder expectations. Its enablers are about customers, internal and external: identify their expectations, align and maintain the outcomes to them, monitor their satisfaction, and respond as needed. On Halcyon the external customers are 212 people on Mars and the families who'd give a great deal to hear them breathe.`,
          `Managing expectations starts with finding out what they are. Then you tell people the truth about what the project will and won't do, and you work on the distance between the two. When you sit down with someone whose expectation you can't meet, the answer you came to give is rarely the most useful thing in the room. What they need instead usually is, and some of it you can do.`,
          `Our families in 2035 got a newsletter. It had a photo of the crew on the front and a paragraph from me, and I signed it every month. I kept copies. I don't think there's a sentence of mine in any of them that would survive one of those families reading it today.`,
          `That's page two. The gate is real. Everything in front of it is a guess. Plan like you know the difference.`],
        terms: [[`Stakeholder expectations`,`What a stakeholder believes the project will deliver or how it will behave, whether stated or not.`],[`Customer satisfaction`,`How well the delivered outcomes meet the customer's expectations, monitored throughout the project.`]],
        exam: `When a stakeholder expects something out of scope, meet them, clarify honestly, and agree on what they can expect. Promising a study to keep them happy only widens the gap.` }
    ],
    video: [`fFluTGvKWMA`,`Work Breakdown Structure (WBS) Explained: Work Package, Control Account, WBS Dictionary`,`PM Aspirant`,`11:29`]
  },
  scenes: [
    { id:`f2s1`, domain:`Process`, task:`R1`, approach:`hybrid`, title:`A pile`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Monday, February 7. Garrett came down to the program room himself at 7:40, which he never did, with a coffee from the place in Bennett and a printout of the board clerk's email. The Independent Readiness Board would meet on March 21 and wanted, the clerk wrote, "the program's integrated project management plan." Someone had underlined integrated twice, and it hadn't been Garrett.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Garrett">"So send them the plan."</span>`,`Quinn had pulled together everything that might count. The integrated master schedule, thirty one thousand lines. A risk management plan from 2059 describing a risk review board that had last met in the spring. A quality plan that lived at Aldergrove and referred to Tallgrass as "the customer." Rafa's release plan, which was a board in his team's tool covered in colored notes. The procurement terms, which were inside four contracts. The communication plan for the Breather work. Nothing joined any of them to any of the others. Nobody's name was on the whole thing, because there wasn't a whole thing.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Quinn">"It's eleven documents and a board,"</span> she said. <span class="said" data-who="Quinn">"I don't think it's a plan. I think it's a pile."</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Own it himself: each lead writes their own section, and he joins those, the baselines, and the seams into one plan.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:3,health:5},
          after:`Sam puts every lead's name on a one page table, with the section each one owns and the date it's due. Then he and Quinn spend two evenings on the part nobody owns, the joints. Where the risk plan's reviews feed the schedule. Where Aldergrove's quality plan has to hand its test data to Wendell. Where Rafa's release plan lands on the hardware's red dates. They find thirty six places where two documents assume different things. Hana reads the list and says, <span class="said" data-who="Hana">"Thirty six. I'd have bet forty."</span> It's the first joke she's made anywhere near him in a week, and she still doesn't quite look at him while she makes it.`,
          why:`The integrated plan joins the subsidiary plans and the baselines into one document that says how the project gets run, watched, and closed. The leads own their parts. You own the whole, and the seams most of all, because a gap between two good plans is still a gap. Then you keep it current.`},
        {t:`Make Quinn the owner of the integrated plan, since she built the schedule and knows where every piece of it lives.`, s:1, d:{trust:1,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`Quinn says yes. She always says yes. Within a week she's chasing leads twice her age for their sections, and they answer her when they get around to it. Hana says propulsion's section is in work. Rafa sends a link. When two pieces of the plan disagree, Quinn has no standing to settle it, so she writes the disagreements in a list and brings it to Sam, which is what would have happened anyway, a week later.`,
          why:`Quinn can build most of it, and she should. But the integrated plan belongs to the project manager, because the seams between its pieces are decisions, and decisions need somebody with the authority to make them. Delegate the work. Keep the ownership.`},
        {t:`Send the board the integrated master schedule, since every activity in the program already sits in its thirty one thousand lines.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-5,health:-2},
          after:`The clerk writes back the same afternoon. The board has received a schedule. It asked for a plan. She attaches the board's checklist, which has nineteen lines on it, and the schedule satisfies one of them.`,
          why:`A schedule says when. It doesn't say how risk gets reviewed, what quality means, who decides a change, or how anybody will know the work is done. The schedule is one part of the plan, and treating it as the whole is the oldest shortcut there is.`},
        {t:`Bring in the consulting firm Garrett uses to write a complete plan in six weeks, so the leads can stay on the hardware.`, s:0, d:{trust:-3,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`The consultants are polite and expensive and spend four weeks interviewing people who'd rather be working. The plan they hand over is two hundred pages and very handsome. It describes a program a bit like Halcyon and a lot like whatever they wrote a plan for last. Hana finds the word "pharmaceutical" on page 61.`,
          why:`A plan written by people who won't carry it out describes somebody else's project. The leads have to write their own parts, because the plan is how they agree to work together. Help is fine. Outsourcing the agreement isn't.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f2s2`, domain:`People`, task:`P4`, approach:`predictive`, title:`Behind the tank`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Tuesday, February 8. Hana had missed Monday's leads meeting with a calendar note that said stand. She'd missed Tuesday's standup with a note that said stand. Wendell, asked, said she'd spent both days out there running leak checks that didn't need her.`,`Sam had the stand file on his laptop and the pressure trace printed and folded in his jacket. He had the feeling, which he didn't like, that the whole building knew what was in his jacket.`,`Rafa caught him in the hall by the vending machines. <span class="said" data-who="Rafa">"Whatever it is, she's been carrying it since before you got here,"</span> he said, which was more than Rafa usually said about anyone on the hardware side. <span class="said" data-who="Rafa">"Just so you know."</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Put the stand traces up at Wednesday's leads meeting and ask propulsion to explain the 2059 downgrade to everyone.`, s:0, d:{trust:-6,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`He puts the trace on the wall screen. Hana explains the ground fault in a flat, complete voice, the way you'd give a deposition. Nobody else says a word. Afterward the leads go back to their desks, and for the rest of the month every one of them checks with propulsion before telling Sam anything about propulsion.`,
          why:`You turned a question into a trial with her team watching. Even if the downgrade was wrong, the person who made it deserves to hear your evidence first, and in private. Build the trust, then use the process, in that order.`},
        {t:`Get the raw 2059 data from Wendell and from the valve supplier first, so he has the whole story before he talks to her.`, s:1, d:{trust:-1,conf:1,health:2},
          after:`Wendell gives him everything, and Aldergrove sends its own test logs within a day. By Friday Sam knows more about August 18, 2059 than anyone but the people who were there. When he finally sits down with Hana, she already knows he's been asking around. She answers every question exactly and volunteers nothing at all.`,
          why:`Doing your homework is fine, and you'll need that data. But going around someone to collect evidence about them tells them where they stand with you. Talk to the person, then gather the rest together.`},
        {t:`Drive out to the stand, find her alone, show her the trace, and ask her to tell him what she saw back then.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:6,conf:1,health:3},
          after:`He finds her at dusk on the stand's upper deck, out of the wind behind the oxidizer tank, with her hood up and her hands in her sleeves. He gives her the printout. She holds it flat against the tank so it won't blow away. He asks what that August was like, and then he shuts up. She talks for a long time about the ground fault, three channels spiking for months, her and Wendell chasing it through every connector on two racks with flashlights in their teeth. <span class="said" data-who="Hana">"I believed it was noise. I still mostly believe it."</span> A gust takes the end of the sentence, and she waits for it to pass. <span class="said" data-who="Hana">"Mostly is a terrible word for a valve."</span> Below them a pronghorn crosses the access road, stops, and goes on. Sam asks if there's anything she wants him to know before he decides. <span class="said" data-who="Hana">"If you open it, don't do it to me in a meeting."</span> He tells her he won't. She looks at him then, for about a second, and hands the trace back folded smaller than he gave it to her.`,
          why:`Engaging a stakeholder starts with listening. Take the evidence to the person first, privately, and try to understand what they saw and why they decided what they did. Code 3.2.3 asks you to listen to other points of view, seeking to understand them. The trust you build now is what lets the formal process work later.`},
        {t:`Ask Garrett who pushed for the downgrade back in 2059, so he knows the politics before he raises it with anyone.`, s:0, d:{trust:-5,conf:-1,health:0},
          after:`Garrett laughs. <span class="said" data-who="Garrett">"Nobody pushed anything. People made a call. That's what engineers do."</span> By the end of the day Hana has heard from two people that Sam went to the CEO about her. She doesn't come in Wednesday.`,
          why:`You went to the top for gossip about someone who works for you, before you'd talked to her. Whatever the politics are, you'll learn them better from the work itself. Start with the person closest to the decision.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f2s3`, domain:`Business Environment`, task:`B5`, approach:`predictive`, title:`Reason for reopening`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Thursday, February 10. Sam had spent two days learning the anomaly process the way he'd once learned a new jet's tech orders, from the front. Anyone could open an anomaly report. Only a disposition could close one: explained and fixed, explained and accepted, or, when nobody could find a cause and the thing didn't happen again, UNR. Unexplained, not repeatable. Reopening a closed report took the program manager's signature and nothing else.`,`Nothing else on paper. Off paper, AR 2207 had been closed for seventeen months, and every test on the departure stage since had been built on the valve being fine. Quinn ran the numbers at lunch. A real investigation, if it came to a new test campaign, could cost the stand three to eight weeks, and the stand was already on the critical path.`,`At 4:30 Hana sent him one line. <b>Whatever you decide, I'll run it straight.</b>`,`The form was on his screen with the cursor blinking in the box that said REASON FOR REOPENING. It was 5:15, the light outside was going orange over the prairie, and the clock over the high bay doors said 738.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Leave it closed. It was reviewed and signed, two retests were clean, and reopening it now costs weeks nobody has.`, s:0, flag:`buriedar`, d:{trust:-3,conf:1,health:-7},
          after:`He closes the form without saving it. The next morning he moves Bea's folder into an archive directory, and he notices himself doing it, the way you notice your hand moving toward something hot. Out at the stand Wendell runs the week's leak checks and doesn't ask. Hana doesn't ask either. The valve sits in its blanket, closed on paper, exactly the way it was on Monday.`,
          why:`Two retests on the same stand, the same afternoon, with the same ground fault aren't independent evidence of anything, and you know the closure came eleven days before a review. When we discover an error or omission, by ourselves or others, Code 2.2.5 asks for prompt corrective action. Leaving it closed is accepting a crit 1 risk without saying so.`},
        {t:`Reopen it at criticality 1 in the formal anomaly process, with Hana's team on the technical work and the board told.`, s:3, best:true, flag:`r_reopened`, d:{trust:3,conf:2,health:8},
          after:`He types the reason in one sentence. <b>Closure rationale not supported by independent evidence; failure mode is criticality 1 by consequence.</b> He signs it. Then he calls Hana before the system can email her, so she hears it from him, and tells her the investigation is hers to run, with Wendell. <span class="said" data-who="Hana">"Okay,"</span> she says. Then, after a while, <span class="said" data-who="Hana">"Thank you for calling first."</span> At 5:40 he sends the board's clerk a one paragraph notice. At 5:52 Garrett calls. Sam lets it ring, opens the trace, and calls him back at six and walks him through it. Garrett listens to the whole thing. <span class="said" data-who="Garrett" data-tone="Cold anger, quick and hard.">"That's three to eight weeks."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Sam" data-tone="Quiet, steady, unapologetic.">"Maybe."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Garrett" data-tone="Cold anger, quick and hard.">"You know what you're doing to me."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Sam" data-tone="Quiet, steady, unapologetic.">"Yes."</span> Garrett hangs up without saying goodbye, which with Garrett isn't the worst thing that can happen.`,
          why:`A closed report with a doubt behind it is an unmanaged risk with a signature on it. Reopening it puts it back where the process can see it, own it, and work it. Give it to the people who know the hardware, tell the person who made the original call before anyone else, and tell governance. Identify the risk honestly, then let the process do its job.`},
        {t:`Leave it closed on paper for now, and have Wendell take a quiet look at the data so nobody panics before there's a finding.`, s:1, d:{trust:1,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`Wendell takes the look. He's thorough and he's quiet, and by the end of February there are forty pages of printouts in a drawer of Sam's desk that nobody else knows exist. That's the trouble with quiet. The investigation has no owner on paper, no budget, no place in the schedule, and no way for the board to know it's happening.`,
          why:`Your instinct to look harder is right. Looking off the books isn't. An investigation outside the process can't get resources, can't change the plan, and can't be seen by the governance that's supposed to decide crit 1 risks. Quiet doesn't calm anybody down for long. Open it formally.`},
        {t:`Ask Aldergrove to recertify the valve in writing and attach the letter to the closed report, so the file is complete.`, s:0, d:{trust:0,conf:1,health:-4},
          after:`Aldergrove's account lead calls him herself, which surprises him. She says they'll certify that the valve met its acceptance criteria on the two clean cycles, because it did, and she wants him to know that's all the letter will say. <span class="said" data-who="Nell">"You're asking me for a piece of paper, Mr. Okafor. We've been somebody's piece of paper before."</span> The letter comes Monday. It says exactly that much.`,
          why:`A supplier's letter certifies what the supplier tested. It doesn't explain a 40 millisecond event nobody ever explained. Adding paper to a closed file makes the file thicker without making the risk any smaller. Reopen it and find out.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f2s4`, domain:`Process`, task:`R2`, approach:`hybrid`, title:`One box with a door`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Monday, February 14. Valentine's Day, which Sam remembered at 6:50 AM because the grocery store in Bennett had a rack of red balloons by the door and he stood looking at it longer than a man buying coffee should. Grace had liked the cheap ones, the kind that went soft by Wednesday.`,`By eight he was in the program room for the argument the integrated plan had turned up. Bea's work breakdown structure covered the hardware down to the fasteners: three modules, the Breather, ground support, the launch campaign, a little over four hundred work packages. For flight software it had one box labeled SOFTWARE, and under it, in Bea's capitals, ASK RAFA.`,`Rafa had come ready. <span class="said" data-who="Rafa">"My scope is a backlog. Eleven hundred items, ranked. The top fifty are ready to build. The bottom four hundred are guesses with titles. If you make me turn that into work packages with dates, I'll do it, and it'll be fiction by the next demo."</span>`,`Hana didn't look up from her laptop. <span class="said" data-who="Hana">"And if the valve commands are a guess with a title, I requalify. Again."</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Put every software feature into the WBS as its own work package with dates, so the scope baseline is complete.`, s:0, d:{trust:-3,conf:1,health:-3},
          after:`Rafa's team spends nine days turning eleven hundred backlog items into work packages. By the end of the next sprint a third of them have changed, because Commander Haddad found things in the demo. Quinn now keeps two versions of the software scope, the baselined one and the real one, and only one of them is any use.`,
          why:`Detailed, dated work packages fit scope you can define up front. Software you're still learning about belongs in a ranked backlog that gets refined every sprint. Forcing it into fixed packages doesn't stop the change. It just makes your baseline wrong every two weeks.`},
        {t:`Keep the WBS and the backlog as separate documents, and have Quinn compare the two at each integration milestone.`, s:1, d:{trust:2,conf:0,health:-1},
          after:`It's tidy and everybody can live with it. The first integration milestone is in June. In June Quinn finds that the backlog has grown a guidance feature that needs a sensor nobody put in the WBS, and the sensor has a nine month lead time.`,
          why:`You kept the right tools and lost the connection between them. In a hybrid plan the backlog lives inside the scope, under the WBS element it delivers, so a change on one side shows up on the other while there's still time to act on it.`},
        {t:`Use the WBS for hardware down to work packages, with flight software as one element whose content is Rafa's backlog.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:5,conf:2,health:5},
          after:`Quinn redraws the software box. Under it go the release milestones, tied to the hardware's red dates, and one line that points to Rafa's backlog as the element's content. Any backlog item that needs hardware has to name the WBS element it touches. Rafa reads it twice. <span class="said" data-who="Rafa">"So my whole team is one box."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Sam">"One box with a door in it."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Hana">"Does the door lock?"</span> <span class="said" data-who="Sam">"On the red dates."</span> Rafa thinks about it and says that's fair, which is the first time anyone in the room has heard him use the word.`,
          why:`In a hybrid project the WBS can hold the whole scope while part of it stays adaptive. Decompose the hardware into work packages you can estimate and baseline. Put the software in as an element whose content is the ranked backlog, and tie its releases to the integration dates. One scope, two ways of managing it, and a seam somebody owns.`},
        {t:`Move the hardware into Rafa's tool as epics, so the whole ship is ranked by value and planned the same way, sprint by sprint.`, s:0, d:{trust:-4,conf:0,health:-5},
          after:`The departure stage becomes an epic called DEPARTURE STAGE. Its first story reads "As a crew, we want a stage that doesn't explode." Hana reads it, shuts her laptop, and walks out. She's back ten minutes later with the stage's pressure test procedure, all three hundred and ten printed pages, and sets it down on Rafa's keyboard.`,
          why:`A pressure vessel's scope is known, its changes are expensive, and its tests take weeks. That's predictive work, and it wants a WBS and a baseline. Putting everything in one tool because one team likes the tool is the opposite of tailoring.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f2s5`, domain:`Process`, task:`R2`, approach:`predictive`, title:`Page eleven`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Wednesday, February 16. The scope statement was eleven pages, and the first ten were easy, because they said what the ship would do. Page eleven was the exclusions, and it was blank except for a sticky note Bea had left on the draft. <b>DO THIS PART LAST. EVERYONE WILL HATE IT.</b>`,`Quinn had pulled the items together from the 2058 requirements, the mass budget, and every meeting where somebody had asked for something and been told no. The biggest one was a single paragraph.`,`<prop type="doc" title="HALCYON PROJECT SCOPE STATEMENT · DRAFT 3 · SECTION 11, EXCLUSIONS">11.1 Earth return. Halcyon carries no ascent vehicle and no return propellant. It will not bring any colonist, or its own crew, back to Earth. The crew of four remains at Ares Station until a return opportunity in a later window.<br>11.2 Station expansion beyond the Breather, spare stacks, seed stock, and medical cargo.<br>11.3 A second lander.<br>11.4 Personal cargo above 2 kg per colonist.</prop>`,`Hale had read the draft over the weekend. Her note beside 11.1 was in blue ink: <b>Do we have to say this?</b> Garrett's, under hers: <b>Call it "future capability." Investors like future.</b>`,`Commander Yusra Haddad had four kids in Aurora. She'd read 11.1 too. She hadn't written anything.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Keep 11.1 as written, file the statement, and let the families and the crew find it there when they read the scope.`, s:1, d:{trust:-2,conf:-1,health:2},
          after:`It's accurate, and it's filed. Two weeks later a crew spouse finds it on page eleven of a document on the ARA's procurement site and calls Commander Haddad at the training center in tears. Haddad calls Sam. Her voice is very even. <span class="said" data-who="Yusra" data-tone="Even, cold, hurt.">"I knew. My husband didn't. You put my husband on page eleven."</span>`,
          why:`Writing the exclusion down is right. Filing it and waiting is a different thing from getting agreement. Scope gets defined, then agreed with the stakeholders it affects, and the hardest exclusions deserve to be said out loud to the people they hurt before those people read them.`},
        {t:`Rename it "future capability," as Garrett suggests, so the statement stays accurate and nobody has to give up hope.`, s:0, d:{trust:-3,conf:-5,health:-1},
          after:`"Future capability" goes into draft four. By March the phrase is on Simon Greaves's feed, where a retired ARA engineer explains on camera that there's no such capability, future or otherwise, and asks who wrote the sentence. Frances Odum asks the same question at the next families briefing, more slowly.`,
          why:`An exclusion written to sound like an inclusion is a half-truth, and Code 5.3.1 is explicit about those. A scope statement exists so everyone agrees on the boundary. Blurring it to manage feelings means the feelings arrive later, with interest.`},
        {t:`Leave the exclusions page out of this draft, and settle 11.1 with Hale later, once the baseline is safely approved.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-4,health:-2},
          after:`The statement goes out at ten pages. The board's clerk sends it back the next morning with one question in the cover email, where are the exclusions, and Hale's office replies that they're in work. The families' lawyer reads everything the ARA posts. Her letter arrives Monday and asks, in a single paragraph, whether in work means the ship might bring their children home.`,
          why:`Leaving out what you won't deliver lets everyone assume you will. Exclusions are part of the scope statement because they're where expectations go wrong. Define them, say them, and get them agreed.`},
        {t:`Keep 11.1 plain, with its reason, and take it to Hale and to Commander Haddad in person to agree it before it's filed.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:5,conf:4,health:3},
          after:`He takes Hale the mass budget and the reason in one sentence: the window gives them exactly enough lift for the Breather and the people to install it, and not one ascent vehicle more. <span class="said" data-who="Imogen">"If I sign that, I'm on record saying we're leaving four people on Mars."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Sam">"You're on record either way, ma'am. This way it's in your words."</span> She signs it Friday. Haddad reads it in the gym at the training center in Aurora, still sweaty, feet up on a weight bench, and then reads it again. <span class="said" data-who="Yusra" data-tone="Quiet, steady, serious.">"I signed up for one way in 2059. I'd like my husband to hear it from you before he reads it."</span> On Saturday Sam sits at the Haddads' kitchen table for an hour. Four kids are being quiet on purpose in the next room, badly, and the youngest keeps coming in for water.`,
          why:`Define the scope, including what it won't do, and get agreement from the stakeholders it affects most. Plain words and a reason make the boundary something people can accept, or argue with honestly. Exclusions are where projects come apart later, so settle them on paper now.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f2s6`, domain:`Process`, task:`R2`, approach:`predictive`, title:`The last four hundred meters`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Friday, February 18, 2061. Two years to the day. Somebody on second shift had taped a paper party hat to the countdown clock over the doors, a gold one, a little crushed, and under it the clock said 730.`,`The Breather was the reason for the ship and the least broken down thing in it. Bea's WBS had one element for it, BREATHER (OXYGEN PLANT), with four boxes underneath: stacks, water, power, controls. The electrochemistry team was eleven people in a lab at the far end of the high bay where the air smelled like a swimming pool. Their lead, Owen Talbot, a soft spoken man with a beard he kept trimming shorter as the winter went on, had brought his own breakdown, and it was organized by who sat where.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Owen">"These are my four groups,"</span> he said. <span class="said" data-who="Owen">"That's the work."</span>`,`Quinn drew his four boxes on the whiteboard. Under them she sketched the lander, and off to the right the station, and ran a line from one to the other. Then she stood there with the cap of the marker in her teeth and didn't say what the line was for.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Keep the breakdown by group, stacks, water, power, controls, since that's how the Breather team is organized and staffed.`, s:0, d:{trust:1,conf:0,health:-5},
          after:`The groups know their boxes, and nobody has to move desks. In April somebody asks which group owns getting the Breather out of the lander and into the plant room, and four leads name four different groups, one of them with total confidence. In May somebody asks who's training the station's engineers to run it. Nobody is.`,
          why:`A WBS breaks down deliverables. Organized by who sits where, it hides whatever falls between the groups. Break the product down into the things that have to exist, then give each one an owner.`},
        {t:`Break it down by deliverable with the team, to estimable packages, and check that the pieces add up to all of it.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:2,health:7},
          after:`Two afternoons, most of the whiteboard, and a second box of markers. Plant hardware, yes, and also everything that has to exist before the plant can breathe for anybody: the install kit, the procedures, training for the station's engineers, a cutover from the old plant with no gap in it, the spares, and the test that proves the whole thing works at Mars pressure. When they add the pieces back up, Quinn finds what nobody owned. The last four hundred meters, from the lander to the plant room, across the regolith, at sixty below. <span class="said" data-who="Owen">"I thought the station did that."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Quinn">"The station thought we did."</span> They write a WBS dictionary entry for every package, and Owen trims his beard again that weekend, which Quinn says is a good sign.`,
          why:`Decompose the scope by deliverable, with the people who'll do the work, down to work packages small enough to estimate and own. Then apply the 100 percent rule: the children have to add up to all of the parent and nothing outside it. That check is how you find the work that falls between teams.`},
        {t:`Break it down to the hour, every task the Breather team will do, so the work is visible and can be tracked every day.`, s:0, d:{trust:-3,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`By the end of March the Breather breakdown is six thousand lines long and takes one engineer every Thursday to update. You can find any hour in it. You can't find the plant. And none of the hours belong to the four hundred meters between the lander and the plant room, since nobody ever booked time against them.`,
          why:`Work packages should be small enough to estimate and manage, and no smaller. Breaking down to the hour turns a plan into a timesheet, and it still misses the work nobody thought of. Decompose the deliverables and check the whole.`},
        {t:`Use the Breather statement of work as the breakdown, since it already lists every deliverable the contract requires.`, s:1, d:{trust:1,conf:1,health:1},
          after:`It's a good list, and it matches what Tallgrass owes the ARA line for line. What the station has to do isn't on it. Neither is the trip from the lander to the plant room. No contract mentions either one, so the SOW can't.`,
          why:`Starting from the statement of work is reasonable. But the WBS covers all of the project's work, including work no contract names. Use the SOW as an input, and decompose with the team until the whole thing adds up.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f2s7`, domain:`Business Environment`, task:`B5`, approach:`hybrid`, title:`Line 371`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>{const re=s.flags.r_reopened||G.r_reopened, bu=s.flags.buriedar||G.buriedar, p=(s.picks||{})[2];
        return [`Tuesday, February 22. Quinn had printed the risk register, because she said she couldn't think about it on a screen. Four hundred and twelve lines in eight point type, stapled at the corner, sorted by score the way it had always been sorted, highest first.`,`It took Sam twenty minutes to find the valve. It was on page nine.`,`<prop type="doc" title="HALCYON RISK REGISTER · LINE 371 OF 412">R-0388 · DEPARTURE STAGE · MOIV-1 POSITION INDICATION ANOMALY (REF AR 2207)<br>LIKELIHOOD 1 (RARE) · IMPACT 3 (CRIT 2) · SCORE 3<br>OWNER: PROPULSION · RESPONSE: ACCEPT · STATUS: CLOSED WITH AR</prop>`,
        (re?`AR 2207 had been open again for twelve days. Its line in the register still said closed.`
          :bu?`AR 2207 was closed, and so was its line in the register. Sam had decided that. He read the line anyway.`
          :p===2?`AR 2207 was closed on paper, and Wendell's quiet look was in a drawer. The register knew about neither one.`
          :`AR 2207 was closed, with Aldergrove's letter attached to it now. The register hadn't noticed either thing.`),
        `Directly above it, at line 370, was the risk that the program's coffee service contract would lapse. Score 4.`,`Sam read the top twenty. Most of them were written the way registers get written by tired people. Schedule pressure on stage integration. Thermal margin concerns. Nothing in any of them said what would happen to whom. He'd spent nine years on a flight line where the forms used plain words because the people reading them were nineteen and exhausted, and the plain words were part of what kept the nineteen year olds alive.`];},
      opts:[
        {t:`Rate it crit 1 by consequence, keep likelihood separate, give it an owner and a trigger, and write it in plain words.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:3,health:6},
          after:`Quinn and Sam spend the afternoon rewriting the top forty lines in one form, his flight line form: if this happens, then this, and this is who gets hurt. The valve line becomes <b>If MOIV-1 fails to close on command at departure burn cutoff, the stage overburns, and the crew of four and the colony's air end up on a trajectory that doesn't reach Mars.</b> Severity: crit 1. Likelihood: unknown, written as unknown. Owner: Hana. Trigger: any position disagreement, on any cycle, anywhere. Sorted by severity first, it's on page one. Wendell reads the new top forty standing at Quinn's desk and doesn't say anything for a while. Then he says, <span class="said" data-who="Wendell">"That's the first one of these I ever read all the way through."</span>`,
          why:`Identify and analyze risks honestly, and keep the two axes apart: impact comes from the consequence, likelihood from the evidence. If the likelihood is unknown, write unknown. Every risk needs an owner, a response, and a trigger, and the register has to be written so a tired person can read it and know what's at stake.`},
        {t:`Leave the valve line at crit 2, as the closed report says, so the register matches the program's official records.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:0,health:-6},
          after:`The register matches the records. It also tells the board, every month, that the main valve on the departure stage worries the program less than the coffee contract. When Quinn sends the monthly top ten to the ARA, the valve isn't on it, and it never will be.`,
          why:`A register that copies a disputed rating forward only tells you what somebody decided once. Severity comes from the consequence of the failure mode. If the consequence is losing the crew or the vehicle, the register says so, whatever a closed report says.`},
        {t:`Keep the valve out of the register. An anomaly belongs in the anomaly system, and two records of one item means drift.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`The anomaly system and the register both stay tidy. The trouble is that the register is what goes to the board, and the anomaly system isn't. The monthly top ten goes to the ARA without the valve on it, and the board, which reads the top ten, has no idea there's anything to ask about.`,
          why:`Avoiding duplicate records is a real concern, which is why this isn't a zero. But an anomaly report and a risk do different jobs. The report records what happened. The register manages what could happen next and who owns it. Link them. Don't drop one.`},
        {t:`Keep crit 2 but raise the likelihood to 3, so the score climbs into the top fifty without restarting the criticality fight.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:1,health:-3},
          after:`The score goes to 9, and the line moves up to page three, just under thermal margin concerns. It's visible now and still wrong: a failure mode that can lose the crew, filed as a mission inconvenience with a medium chance, a number nobody can defend in either direction.`,
          why:`You fixed the ranking by bending the wrong axis. Likelihood comes from evidence and severity from consequence, and trading one for the other to get a better sort order hides both. Rate each one honestly.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f2s8`, domain:`Business Environment`, task:`B2`, approach:`predictive`, title:`Four times`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>{const re=s.flags.r_reopened||G.r_reopened;
        return [`Thursday, February 24. Every requirement that touched the program lived in a compliance matrix, nine thousand rows, kept by an ARA mission assurance office in Denver that Sam had met exactly once. Quinn had spent two nights in it. After the register she'd wanted to know one thing: whether AR 2207 was the only report on Halcyon that had ever been downgraded.`,`It wasn't. There were four. All four had started at criticality 1 and been revised to criticality 2 with a rationale about test conditions, and each of them had closed in the month before a schedule review. Because each one was crit 2 on the day it closed, Flight Rule 2.36 showed against all four as N/A.`,
        (re?`AR 2207 was open again. The other three weren't. They were small things on the departure stage and the habitat: a heater, a pressure switch, a hatch latch.`
          :`The other three were small things on the departure stage and the habitat: a heater, a pressure switch, a hatch latch. AR 2207 was the fourth.`),
        `<span class="said" data-who="Quinn">"None of them broke the rule,"</span> Quinn said. She had her hands flat on the table. <span class="said" data-who="Quinn">"That's what I can't stop looking at. Nobody broke it. They went around it the same way four times."</span>`];},
      opts:[
        {t:`Record that all four closed properly at crit 2, so the rule's letter was met, and leave the matrix showing N/A.`, s:0, d:{trust:-4,conf:-1,health:-5},
          after:`The matrix stays clean. On paper the program is in full compliance, four times over, with a rule written to stop exactly this. Quinn stops bringing him the things she finds in the matrix, because the last thing she brought him turned out not to count.`,
          why:`A rule written to keep crit 1 anomalies from closing as unexplained is defeated by downgrading them first, and four times is a pattern. When you find a threat to compliance, you measure it and report it. Certifying the workaround makes you part of it.`},
        {t:`Restore crit 1 on all four, brief ARA mission assurance, and keep the board out of it until the reports are clean.`, s:1, d:{trust:1,conf:-2,health:3},
          after:`Mission assurance is grateful and quick. The three small reports go back to crit 1 and get real dispositions on the calendar. But the board, which owns crit 1 risk decisions, hears about the pattern in April from mission assurance, secondhand, and asks the program in writing why it didn't say so itself.`,
          why:`Fixing the records is real corrective action, which is why this isn't a zero. But the independent board is the governance for these decisions, and keeping it out until things look better is managing its opinion. Report the pattern to the people with the authority to act on it.`},
        {t:`Ask the ARA for a standing waiver of Rule 2.36 on the departure stage, so the schedule can't be held hostage by it.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-6,health:-4},
          after:`Hale's chief of staff reads the request and calls him. <span class="said" data-who="woman">"You want to waive the Petrel rule. On the departure stage. In writing."</span> There's a pause. <span class="said" data-who="woman">"I'm going to pretend I didn't get this, and you're going to unsend it."</span>`,
          why:`A waiver is a fair tool for a requirement that doesn't fit, after analysis, with the consequences understood. Asking for one to protect a schedule from a safety rule runs it backward. Analyze the consequences of noncompliance first. Here they're the reason the rule exists.`},
        {t:`Check every crit 1 downgrade against the rule's intent, report the pattern to the board, and propose that it must concur.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:5,health:5},
          after:`Quinn pulls every downgrade back to 2058. There are four, and only these four. Sam sends the board a two page memo that names the reports, the dates, the reviews they came before, and what each rationale rested on. It names no people. At the bottom it proposes one control: no crit 1 report on Halcyon gets downgraded without the board's written concurrence. The clerk logs it as Question 61-04 for the March session. The next morning the clerk's acknowledgment comes back with the memo stamped and numbered, and in the margin, beside the stamp, a small tick in green ink. <b>Docketed. R.C.</b> Sam looks at the two letters for a while before he files it.`,
          why:`Confirm the compliance requirements, look for threats to them, and measure how far the project really complies. A workaround used four times is a threat to compliance even when every instance met the letter of the rule. Report it to the governance that owns the rule and propose a control that closes the gap. Code 2.3.1 asks you to uphold the rules that govern your work, and that includes what they're for.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f2s9`, domain:`People`, task:`P6`, approach:`hybrid`, title:`The valve lady`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>{const rosy=s.flags.rosylog||G.rosylog, re=s.flags.r_reopened||G.r_reopened, bu=s.flags.buriedar||G.buriedar;
        return [`Monday, February 28. 5:40 AM. One way light time this morning was six minutes and twenty eight seconds, the shortest it had been since Ada landed. In April it would bottom out a little over five, and Quinn had put a note on the program calendar that said CLOSEST, with no explanation, as if everyone would know what it meant. Everyone did.`,
        `<prop type="doc" kind="vlog" from="Ada" sol="3565" delay="6 min 28 s" margin="911 days" title="ADA OKAFOR · ARES STATION · SOL 3565 · ONE WAY 6 MIN 28 S">Hi, Dad. Sol 3565. I have tomatoes. Eleven of them, on the Bay C vines, red. Actually red. You don't know what red does to a person who's looked at orange dirt for two years. I'm cutting one into twelve pieces for the shift tonight, and Dr. Lindgren gets a piece, because she finally stopped asking about the basil.<br><br>`+
          (rosy?`Your last log. You said on track. You said it twice, which is once more than you say things you believe. I'm not going to fight with you from here. I'm going to grow tomatoes at you until you tell me.`
            :`Your last log. I watched it twice, like always. You look tired in that kitchen light. It's the bulb over the table, Dad. It's been dying since I was in high school. Change the bulb.`)+
          `<br><br>Okay. Real stuff. We had our first ration cut four sols ago. Hollis calls it a consumption adjustment. Calories down a tenth, three gym sessions a week instead of six, and the rec module goes dark at 2100 so we'll sleep more and breathe less. It's very scientific. It's also everybody hungry and in bed early, like a summer camp nobody signed up for. Hollis's report says it buys fifteen days. I ran it off the plant log, same as before. I get eight. So my number's 911 by the time you hear this. I know what you're doing right now. You're doing subtraction on the back of something.<br><br>One more thing, then tomatoes again. Teodor in the machine shop worked for a valve company in Pueblo before he came out. He says there's a woman there who's been arguing about one valve for twenty six years, and everybody at the company calls her the valve lady. He also says, and I'm only telling you what's going around the galley, that a valve on your ship did something weird in a test a couple of years ago and somebody closed the paperwork. Everybody here's heard it by now. Nobody here can check it. That's the bad part, up here. You hear a thing and there's nothing to do with it except hear it again at dinner.<br><br>Have you met the valve lady? If she's real, I want to know what she's been arguing about.<br><br>Remember the chore chart? Color coded, laminated, with a legend. I was nine. Mom said you'd made a Gantt chart for a fourth grader, and you said it was a responsibility matrix, and she laughed so hard she had to sit down on the stairs. I'm running the hydroponics expansion off a chart now. It's laminated. I hate that you were right.<br><br>Love you. 911. Ada out.</prop>`,
        `He got the gas bill out from under the fruit bowl. Under minus seventeen he wrote 911, and under that 920, the days from this morning to September 6, 2063. Minus nine. Eight days better on her number, because two hundred and twelve people were going to bed hungry at nine o'clock. Hollis's report, the one the ARA posted every Monday, would say 978, fifty eight to spare.`,
        (re?`Then the valve. There was an old report. Eighteen days ago he'd reopened it. That was the true answer, and it was the only one he could give her yet, and it wasn't the whole of what she was asking.`
          :bu?`Then the valve. There was an old report. It was closed. He'd left it that way. Every word of that was true, and he could already hear exactly how it would sound in the voice.`
          :`Then the valve. There was an old report, and nobody outside a few people knew what was being done about it, and he couldn't explain that to her without explaining why.`),
        `At 9:20 Frances Odum called his cell. She didn't say hello. <span class="said" data-who="Frances">"Malik's message came in this morning. He says they're on rations and in bed by nine, and Hollis calls it an adjustment."</span> A breath. <span class="said" data-who="Frances">"Families of Ares has a call Thursday. Two hundred people. Most of them have one question. They've heard the ship won't bring anybody home, and they want to know if it's true. Some of them have sick kids up there, Mr. Okafor. They want a seat."</span>`];},
      opts:[
        {t:`Go on the families' call himself, say plainly what Halcyon can't do and why, and ask them what they need from him.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:6,health:2},
          after:`Thursday he sits in front of a laptop in the program room with two hundred faces on the screen and Frances in the corner tile. He says it the way it's written: nobody comes home on Halcyon. He says why, with the mass budget, in one sentence. Then he stops talking and asks what they need. It takes forty minutes. Most of them don't need a seat they know isn't there. They need the program's real forecast every month, with the odds. They need somebody at the program who answers email. They need to know which medicines are in the cargo. He writes it all down and commits to all three that week, with the email as his own job. At the end Frances says, <span class="said" data-who="Frances">"That's the first time anybody from Tallgrass has told us something we didn't want to hear."</span> She doesn't say thank you. She doesn't hang up first, either.`,
          why:`Managing expectations starts with finding out what they are. Then you align what the project will deliver with them, out loud, bad news included. Here the customers are the colonists and their families. Telling them what's excluded and asking what they need turns a fight over a seat into a list you can do something about.`},
        {t:`Tell Frances the program will study a medical return option, so the families have something hopeful to hold on to.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-6,health:-1},
          after:`The families hear Thursday that a medical return option is being studied. By the weekend it's on Greaves's feed as Halcyon may bring sick colonists home. Ten days later the ARA has to issue a correction, and the correction is what everybody remembers.`,
          why:`Promising a study you know can't succeed sets an expectation the project can't meet. Hope you manufacture now gets paid back later, out of trust. Tell people what's true, and work on what's possible.`},
        {t:`Send Frances a careful written answer on what Halcyon won't carry and why, for her to read out on Thursday's call.`, s:1, d:{trust:1,conf:-1,health:0},
          after:`The letter is careful and accurate. Frances reads it aloud on Thursday to two hundred people, and in her voice it sounds like a letter. The first question afterward is whether anybody from the program was willing to come. Frances says no.`,
          why:`Putting the facts in writing is good, and accurate beats kind. But expectations this painful get aligned in conversation, where people can ask the next question. Show up, then follow up in writing.`},
        {t:`Have ARA public affairs take the families' call, since it's the agency's mission and its policy to answer for it.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-4,health:0},
          after:`Public affairs sends a woman with a binder. She answers every question with a sentence from it. Frances lets her get through eleven, then asks, on the call, whether the program lead knows his own daughter is on that station. The woman says she can't speak to personnel matters.`,
          why:`Public affairs can help you communicate. It can't find out what the families expect on your behalf, and it can't commit the program to anything. Managing stakeholder expectations is the project's work.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f2s10`, domain:`Business Environment`, task:`B3`, approach:`agile`, title:`Forty million`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Thursday, March 3. Hale came out to Watkins at noon with her chief of staff and a printout, and this time she sat down.`,`<prop type="doc" title="ARA · OFFICE OF THE DIRECTOR · REQUEST">Add an external camera to the habitat module to carry the departure burn and Mars arrival live. Countdown to Ares has offered to stream both free to its audience (40 million). Partners strongly in favor. Needed for the May partner meeting: confirmation that it's in the baseline.</prop>`,`<span class="said" data-who="Imogen">"Forty million people watching that ship leave,"</span> she said. <span class="said" data-who="Imogen">"Do you know what that does to a budget vote?"</span>`,`Rafa, who'd been asked to sit in, said the ship already had six navigation cameras, and he could probably stream two of them with a software change and no new hardware. Hana said probably was doing a lot of work in that sentence, since the navigation cameras fed guidance. Quinn wrote on her pad and turned it so Sam could read it. <b>HAB MASS MARGIN 410 KG. EACH KG ON HAB COSTS ABOUT 2 KG OF PROPELLANT.</b>`,
        ((s.flags.nocharter||G.nocharter)
          ?`There was no charter, so nothing written down said who decided a change like this. Everyone at the table was looking at Sam as if he knew.`
          :`The change process written into the plan in February named a change board: Sam, the ARA's mission assurance lead, and Hale's deputy, with anything over a threshold going up to Hale. Hale was the sponsor. Today she was also the person asking.`)],
      opts:[
        {t:`Tell Hale yes. She's the sponsor, it's small next to the ship, and public support is what pays for everything else.`, s:0, d:{trust:-3,conf:2,health:-5},
          after:`Hale leaves happy. The next week the habitat team learns from the partner meeting minutes that they're adding a camera, with a heater, a mount, and a cable run nobody has routed, to a module whose thermal analysis closed in December. Hana learns from Rafa that guidance may be sharing a data bus with a television show.`,
          why:`A sponsor's request is still a change request. Approving it at the table skips the impact analysis, the decision process, and the people whose work it changes. Analyze it, decide it through change control, and the sponsor gets an answer she can defend.`},
        {t:`Put Rafa's software option into his backlog as a feature, since it needs no hardware and an agile team can absorb it.`, s:1, d:{trust:2,conf:2,health:-3},
          after:`Rafa's team builds it in two sprints, and it works in the lab. Then the first integrated simulation runs a departure burn with the stream on, and the guidance computer drops two frames of star tracker data under the load. Nobody on the hardware side knew the change existed.`,
          why:`The software route may well be the right answer, and the backlog is where Rafa's team would do the work. But this change touches the guidance data path, the integration plan, and a commitment to the sponsor. Changes that cross the baseline or the seams go through change control, even when the team doing the work is agile.`},
        {t:`Log a change request, have the leads size both options for mass, data, and schedule, and take it to the change board.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:4,health:5},
          after:`Sam tells Hale it'll be a change request by Friday and a decision in two weeks, with both options on it, and that she'll hear the answer before the partners do. She doesn't love the two weeks. The analysis turns up more than anybody expected. The hardware camera costs 31 kilos, about 62 of propellant, and a thermal rerun. Rafa's option costs no mass, but it needs a bus load test and a guidance requalification, which Hana insists on and Rafa, to his credit, agrees to. The change board approves Rafa's option, conditional on the test, and writes down why. Quinn sends Hale a one page status that afternoon. Hale reads it and says, <span class="said" data-who="Imogen">"You made me wait two weeks for a yes."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Sam">"For a yes you won't have to take back, ma'am."</span>`,
          why:`Every change, from anyone, takes the same path: write it down, analyze its impact on scope, schedule, cost, quality, and risk, decide it at the right level, update the plan, and tell the requester where it stands. A sponsor asking doesn't change the process. It makes doing the process well matter more.`},
        {t:`Turn it down himself. The baseline's about to close, the habitat has no mass to waste, and a ship isn't a studio.`, s:0, d:{trust:0,conf:-5,health:1},
          after:`Hale hears no from a man she drafted seven weeks ago. She takes it to Garrett, who says yes, and the camera arrives in the program as a directive from the contractor's CEO, with no analysis behind it and Sam's name on the refusal.`,
          why:`You may be right about the camera. It still isn't yours to refuse alone, any more than it's Hale's to approve alone. Analyze it, decide it through change control, and tell her the decision. Your opinion belongs in the analysis.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f2s11`, domain:`Process`, task:`R8`, approach:`hybrid`, title:`Float`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>{const re=s.flags.r_reopened||G.r_reopened;
        return [`Wednesday, March 9. 711 on the clock. Quinn's schedule was ready to baseline, which meant it was ready to become the thing every slip would be measured against for two years, which meant everybody wanted to change it today.`,`It had come up from the bottom: the leads' three point estimates from January, the work packages from February, the Breather breakdown, Rafa's release milestones. `+(re?`AR 2207's investigation was in it too, Hana's estimate, three to eight weeks of stand time, sitting on the critical path like a rock in a creek.`:`The valve was in it the way it had always been, as a line marked complete.`)+` On the leads' own numbers, February 18 was still possible and still not likely.`,`Garrett had his own version. Someone in finance had moved the Breather's acceptance test two weeks earlier, into a week the stand looked free. Sam looked at the week. It was the week Wendell had blocked for the stand's seal change, which was written in Wendell's pocket notebook and nowhere else.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Garrett">"Baseline mine,"</span> Garrett said. <span class="said" data-who="Garrett">"It's got float on the 18th. Investors can read float."</span>`];},
      opts:[
        {t:`Baseline Garrett's version. It shows float on the 18th, investors can read it, and the stand question can be solved later.`, s:0, d:{trust:-4,conf:1,health:-6},
          after:`The baseline goes in with float on February 18. In April the stand goes down for its seal change, as Wendell always said it would, and the Breather test slips three weeks against a baseline that never knew the seal change existed. The program's first variance report reads as a failure, and it's Garrett's version that failed, and it's Sam's signature on it.`,
          why:`A baseline is the yardstick for everything after it. Built on a facility that isn't available, it guarantees variances that only measure the plan's fiction. Coordinate the schedule with operations, and baseline what the people doing the work estimated.`},
        {t:`Baseline Quinn's version, hardware in detail and software by release, once the leads agree and Hale approves.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:5,conf:3,health:6},
          after:`Quinn has the leads sign their sections one at a time at her cube, so nobody signs in a crowd. Rafa signs the release milestones and asks for the backlog itself to stay unbaselined, which is the whole point, and Quinn tells him so, and he looks pleased anyway. Hana signs, then asks to see the stand calendar one more time and reads it with her finger. Hale approves the baseline on the 11th with one line: <b>Baselined on the team's estimates. Confidence reported monthly.</b> Garrett wants the float explained to him in person. Halfway down the page he gets to the seal change and stops. <span class="said" data-who="Garrett" data-tone="Sober, quiet, serious.">"Who knew about that?"</span> <span class="said" data-who="Sam">"Wendell."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Garrett" data-tone="Sober, quiet, serious.">"Only Wendell?"</span> <span class="said" data-who="Sam">"Until about ten this morning."</span> Garrett takes his reading glasses off and taps them on the page twice, which is a thing Sam has never seen him do.`,
          why:`Prepare the schedule to fit the approach: hardware in detail, software at the level of releases tied to integration dates, the backlog left free to change. Build it from the estimates of the people doing the work, coordinate it with operations, then baseline it through governance. The baseline should measure the plan you actually have.`},
        {t:`Hold off on any baseline until the open technical questions close, so the program won't need to rebaseline in a month.`, s:1, d:{trust:1,conf:-3,health:-1},
          after:`The program runs another month without a baseline. Every lead reports progress against whatever date they last heard. When the board asks in April for the program's schedule variance, Quinn has to tell it there's nothing to measure variance against.`,
          why:`Not wanting to rebaseline is a fair instinct. But you can't control what you haven't baselined, and there will always be open questions. Baseline what you know, carry the unknowns as risks and reserves, and change the baseline through change control when you have to.`},
        {t:`Baseline every software story too, with dates, so the whole program is measured one way and no one's half is a black box.`, s:0, d:{trust:-3,conf:0,health:-3},
          after:`Eleven hundred stories go into the baseline with dates on them. Two sprints later three hundred have moved, and every move is a variance. Rafa's team spends Friday afternoons writing explanations for stories nobody had planned to build in March anyway.`,
          why:`Schedule to fit the approach. Agile work gets baselined at the release level, with the backlog ranked and free to change underneath. Baselining every story turns normal backlog refinement into a stream of false variances.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f2s12`, domain:`Process`, task:`R1`, approach:`hybrid`, title:`On the lower number`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>{const re=s.flags.r_reopened||G.r_reopened, bu=s.flags.buriedar||G.buriedar;
        return [`Monday, March 21. The Independent Readiness Board met in a federal building downtown, in a room with good chairs, bad coffee, and a seal on the wall nobody looked at. Seven members sat at a curved table. Ruth was at the end, with the green pen.`,`The program's integrated plan was due to the board by noon. It was finished. Sam had read every section of it, and he knew which parts he believed.`,
        (re?`AR 2207 was in it, open, with Hana's investigation on the critical path.`
          :bu?`AR 2207 wasn't in it. It was closed, the plan treated it as closed, and Sam had read that page more than once.`
          :`AR 2207 was in it as closed, which was technically true.`),
        `What bothered him most wasn't the valve. Every date in the plan sat on one assumption, the ARA's oxygen margin model. On that model, arrival on September 6, 2063 left fifty eight days to spare. On the lower number Sam had seen, the one under his fruit bowl, the ship arrived nine days late. Nobody at the agency had yet said on the record which number was right.`,`Quinn had the cover memo open on her laptop. <span class="said" data-who="Quinn">"What do you want it to say?"</span>`];},
      opts:[
        {t:`Send the summary showing the plan supports February 18, and brief the gaps in person if any board member asks.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-3,health:-5},
          after:`The summary is clean. Nobody asks about gaps, because nobody knows there are any. At the end of the session the chair thanks the program for a plan that appears well integrated. Ruth, at the end of the table, writes something with the green pen and doesn't look up.`,
          why:`Assessing a consolidated plan means finding its dependencies and gaps and saying whether it still delivers the value it exists for. A summary that leaves the gaps for somebody to ask about is withholding them. The board can only govern what it can see.`},
        {t:`Hold the plan back until the agency confirms its oxygen model, so the board isn't asked to review a plan on a bad number.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:-3,health:1},
          after:`The March session goes ahead without the program's plan. The chair has the clerk note its absence in the minutes, in one flat sentence that Sam reads four times that night. The plan finally reaches the board in May, along with two months of decisions it was never shown, and the board's first question is why it's seeing them now.`,
          why:`You're right that the oxygen model is the plan's weakest point. But you can submit a plan with a known gap if you name the gap. Holding it back leaves governance with nothing. Send it, flag the assumption, and ask for the verification.`},
        {t:`Send the board everything, the full plan and all thirty one thousand schedule lines, and let it judge the gaps itself.`, s:0, d:{trust:0,conf:-4,health:-1},
          after:`The submission runs to four thousand pages. The board's staff spends most of the week finding the table of contents. In session the chair asks Sam to name the program's three biggest gaps. He does, clearly, in under a minute. She lets that sit, then turns a page. <span class="said" data-who="woman">"Then why are they on page 2,800?"</span>`,
          why:`Transparency isn't volume. The board needs your assessment of the plan, its gaps, its dependencies, and whether it still delivers the value, with the detail behind it for anyone who wants it. Burying the gaps in pages hides them as well as leaving them out would.`},
        {t:`Send the plan with one page on its gaps, led by the oxygen model, and say whether it still gets the air there in time.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:6,health:5},
          after:`The page has five lines. The first says the whole plan depends on the ARA's oxygen margin model, that station engineering data the program has seen runs about two months lower, and that on the lower number the plan arrives nine days late. The board reads it during the morning break. When the session resumes, Ruth asks the only question she asks all day. <span class="said" data-who="Ruth">"Mr. Okafor. On the lower number, does your plan get air there in time?"</span> <span class="said" data-who="Sam">"No, ma'am. Not as it stands."</span> The room goes quiet the way rooms do when somebody has said the thing. The chair asks the ARA's representative when the agency will verify its model. The representative says soon. The chair says May, and writes it down, and so does everybody else at the table.`,
          why:`Assess the consolidated plan for dependencies, gaps, and continued business value, and say what you found. The value here is air at Ares in time. If the plan can't show that on every credible number, the board needs to know in the first paragraph. Name the critical information the plan rests on, and ask for it to be verified.`}
      ]}
  ],
  exercise: { after:`f2s4`, title:`Put it in the WBS or the backlog`, intro:`Rafa and Hana spent a lunch sorting index cards into piles on the program room table, arguing about every third card. Where does each one belong?`,
    items:[
      {prompt:`Fabricate and proof test the Breather's stack manifold assembly.`, choices:[`WBS work package`,`Product backlog item`,`Change request`,`Risk register entry`], answer:0, why:`Defined hardware with acceptance criteria and a fixed test belongs in the WBS as a work package you can estimate, schedule, and baseline.`},
      {prompt:`As the commander, I want the burn display to show valve position in plain words, so I can call a hold without decoding anything.`, choices:[`WBS work package`,`Product backlog item`,`Change request`,`Risk register entry`], answer:1, why:`A user story for flight software. It goes in the ranked backlog under the software element, and Haddad's feedback in the demos refines it.`},
      {prompt:`An external camera on the habitat module, requested by the ARA Director for a live public stream.`, choices:[`WBS work package`,`Product backlog item`,`Change request`,`Risk register entry`], answer:2, why:`It's new scope that touches the baseline and the habitat's mass. It starts as a change request and becomes work only if it's approved.`},
      {prompt:`The main oxidizer isolation valve might fail to close on command at the end of the departure burn.`, choices:[`WBS work package`,`Product backlog item`,`Change request`,`Risk register entry`], answer:3, why:`An uncertain event with a severe consequence is a risk. It goes in the register with an owner, a response, and a trigger. The response may create work later.`},
      {prompt:`Qualification test of the habitat module's pressure shell at the Watkins test stand.`, choices:[`WBS work package`,`Product backlog item`,`Change request`,`Risk register entry`], answer:0, why:`A defined test campaign on defined hardware, with a facility and a fixed duration. Predictive work, in the WBS.`},
      {prompt:`A routine that lets the Breather restart a tripped stack on its own, without waiting twenty minutes for an answer from Earth.`, choices:[`WBS work package`,`Product backlog item`,`Change request`,`Risk register entry`], answer:1, why:`Control software whose details the team will learn with the station's engineers. It's ranked in the backlog under the Breather's software.`},
      {prompt:`Ground support equipment to lift the departure stage onto its shipping fixture for the trip to the Cape.`, choices:[`WBS work package`,`Product backlog item`,`Change request`,`Risk register entry`], answer:0, why:`Easy to forget and fully part of the scope. Ground support equipment is a deliverable, and the 100 percent rule wants it in the WBS.`},
      {prompt:`A crew return vehicle so that sick colonists could ride home on Halcyon.`, choices:[`WBS work package`,`Product backlog item`,`Change request`,`Risk register entry`], answer:2, why:`It's excluded in the scope statement. Adding it would take a change request, with a mass impact the window can't carry.`}
    ]},
  cases: [
    { id:`f2c1`, title:`Case: the light rail extension`, text:[`A city transit agency is building a six mile light rail extension. Track, stations, and vehicles are managed with a predictive plan; a fare and trip planning app is built in two week sprints. During acceptance testing last year, a door interlock on one train failed to hold once, then passed two retests the same afternoon, and the report was closed as not repeatable a week before a funding review. The mayor's office now wants free public wifi on every train by opening day. A neighborhood association still expects a station at its corner, which was dropped from the design two years ago.`] }
  ],
  quiz: [
    {q:`A sponsor asks the project manager for "the plan." The project has a detailed schedule, but its risk, quality, and procurement approaches live in separate documents owned by different leads, and nobody has checked how they fit together. What should the project manager produce?`, opts:[`The detailed schedule, since it holds every planned activity`,`A consolidated plan joining the subsidiary plans and baselines`,`A new plan written by an outside firm to replace the leads' work`,`A summary deck of each lead's document, presented in sequence`], a:1, why:`The integrated project management plan joins the subsidiary plans and the baselines, and the PM owns the seams between them. A schedule is one component, and a plan written by outsiders isn't an agreement among the people doing the work.`, task:`R1`, domain:`Process`, approach:`hybrid`},
    {q:`While reviewing the WBS for a life support unit, the team realizes no element covers installing the unit at the customer's site or training the people who'll run it. What principle was missed?`, opts:[`The 100 percent rule, which requires the WBS to hold all the work`,`Rolling wave planning, which defers the detail until it's needed`,`The critical path method, which links the activities by dependency`,`Progressive elaboration, which adds detail as more becomes known`], a:0, why:`The 100 percent rule says the WBS includes all of the project's work and nothing outside it. Checking that the pieces add up to the whole is how missing work like installation and training gets found.`, task:`R2`, domain:`Process`, approach:`predictive`},
    {q:`Several stakeholders assume a new vessel will be able to bring passengers back, though the design never included it. What should the project manager do?`, opts:[`Leave the topic alone until the stakeholders raise it formally`,`Ask engineering to study the capability to keep stakeholders calm`,`Document the exclusion with its reason and agree it with them`,`Let the sponsor decide privately how much the stakeholders are told`], a:2, why:`Define the scope, including its exclusions, and obtain agreement from the stakeholders it affects. A study meant to soothe them, or silence, only moves the disagreement to a more expensive moment.`, task:`R2`, domain:`Process`, approach:`predictive`},
    {q:`A project builds hardware with stable requirements and software whose features will be refined with users every two weeks. How should the project's scope be organized?`, opts:[`A full WBS with every software feature as a dated work package`,`A product backlog holding the hardware as epics ranked by value`,`Two separate scope documents that are reconciled near the end`,`One WBS, with a ranked backlog under the software element`], a:3, why:`In a hybrid project one WBS can hold the whole scope while the adaptive part is delivered from a ranked backlog under its element, joined to the plan at integration milestones.`, task:`R2`, domain:`Process`, approach:`hybrid`},
    {q:`The team has built a schedule from estimates provided by the people who'll do the work. What should happen before it becomes the schedule baseline?`, opts:[`Key stakeholders review it and it's approved through governance`,`The PM adds two weeks of padding to every critical path activity`,`The PM publishes it to the team and starts tracking against it`,`The CEO adjusts it to match the date promised to the investors`], a:0, why:`A baseline is the approved version of the schedule. After the team builds it and it's coordinated with operations, it's reviewed and approved through governance, and it changes afterward only through change control.`, task:`R8`, domain:`Process`, approach:`predictive`},
    {q:`A failure mode could cause loss of the vehicle, but the event that revealed it happened only once in three tests. A lead proposes lowering its impact rating because it seems unlikely. What should the project manager do?`, opts:[`Lower the impact rating, since the event wasn't repeated in retests`,`Keep impact based on consequence and assess likelihood separately`,`Remove it from the register until the event happens a second time`,`Average the impact and likelihood into a single middle score`], a:1, why:`Impact comes from the consequence of the failure, and probability comes from the evidence. Letting a low probability lower the impact mixes the two axes and hides the risk.`, task:`B5`, domain:`Business Environment`, approach:`predictive`},
    {q:`A compliance review finds that four safety reports were downgraded just below the threshold of a mandatory review rule, each within weeks of a schedule milestone. What should the project manager do?`, opts:[`Restore the original ratings quietly so the records are clean`,`Note that the rule's letter was met, since each was below threshold`,`Request a waiver of the rule to protect the schedule going forward`,`Measure how far it goes and report the pattern to governance`], a:3, why:`A repeated workaround is a threat to compliance even when each instance meets the letter of the rule. Measure the extent, report it to the governance that owns the rule, and propose a control.`, task:`B2`, domain:`Business Environment`, approach:`predictive`},
    {q:`A project manager finds evidence that a respected lead recommended closing a safety report too early. Before raising it formally, what should the project manager do?`, opts:[`Meet the lead privately, share the evidence, and hear their account`,`Present the evidence at the next team meeting so everyone learns`,`Ask other team members what they know before approaching the lead`,`Report the lead to human resources for a formal investigation`], a:0, why:`Build trust by taking the evidence to the person first, privately, and listening to understand their reasoning (Code 3.2.3). Then use the formal process. Public confrontation and going around them both damage the trust the process will need.`, task:`P4`, domain:`People`, approach:`hybrid`},
    {q:`An advocacy group for a project's end users expects the product to include a capability that's outside the approved scope. What should the project manager do?`, opts:[`Promise a feasibility study so the group's expectations stay positive`,`Refer the group to the communications office for a written reply`,`Meet the group, explain the exclusion, and agree on what it can expect`,`Wait until the group asks directly, then send the scope statement`], a:2, why:`Managing expectations means learning what they are, explaining honestly what will and won't be delivered, and aligning on what the stakeholders can expect. A study meant to keep them hopeful widens the gap.`, task:`P6`, domain:`People`, approach:`agile`},
    {q:`Midway through planning, a functional manager refuses to release two engineers, saying no document gives the project manager any authority over shared staff. What is most likely missing?`, opts:[`A signed contract between the two organizations`,`A project charter that the sponsor has issued`,`A responsibility matrix approved by the team`,`A resource histogram for the coming quarter`], a:1, why:`The charter, issued by the sponsor, formally authorizes the project and gives the PM authority to apply organizational resources. A responsibility matrix assigns roles; it doesn't grant authority.`, task:`R4`, domain:`Process`, approach:`predictive`},
    {q:`A distributed team keeps discovering, weeks later, that answers to its questions were misunderstood. What should the project manager add to the communication approach?`, opts:[`More frequent status reports sent out to every team member`,`A rule that every question goes through the project manager`,`A weekly live meeting held at a different time each week`,`A read-back step confirming how each answer was understood`], a:3, why:`A feedback loop that confirms understanding is part of the communication strategy. Without it you've sent information, but you haven't confirmed anyone received it as meant.`, task:`P8`, domain:`People`, approach:`agile`},
    {q:`Six months into a project, new team members can describe their own tasks but not who the project is for. What should the project manager do?`, opts:[`Revisit the vision with the team and tie it to the current work`,`Send the original charter to new members as required reading`,`Add the vision statement to the footer of every status report`,`Ask the sponsor to give a motivational talk at the next all hands`], a:0, why:`A common vision has to be kept current and owned by the people carrying it. Revisiting it with the team, connected to the work in front of them, does that; a document or a speech handed down doesn't.`, task:`P1`, domain:`People`, approach:`hybrid`},
    {q:`In the light rail case, a new project manager reads the closed door interlock report and finds the two retests were run the same afternoon under the same conditions. What should the project manager do?`, opts:[`Leave it closed, since two retests passed and it was formally reviewed`,`Reopen it through the formal process and record the risk by consequence`,`Ask the vehicle supplier to recertify the interlock and attach the letter`,`Have one engineer look into it informally before anyone else is told`], a:1, why:`Same-day retests under the same conditions aren't independent evidence. Reopen the report formally, rate the risk by the consequence of a door failing in service, and let the process own it (Code 2.2.5).`, task:`B5`, domain:`Business Environment`, approach:`predictive`, case:`f2c1`},
    {q:`In the light rail case, the mayor's office asks for public wifi on every train by opening day. What should the project manager do first?`, opts:[`Add it to the app team's backlog, since agile teams welcome change`,`Approve it, since the request comes from a senior stakeholder`,`Analyze its impact and take it through the change control process`,`Decline it, since the vehicle design has already been baselined`], a:2, why:`Every change, whoever asks, gets documented, analyzed for impact, and decided through change control. It touches the vehicles' baseline, so it can't be absorbed quietly by the agile team.`, task:`B3`, domain:`Business Environment`, approach:`hybrid`, case:`f2c1`},
    {q:`In the light rail case, the neighborhood association still expects a station at its corner. What should the project manager do?`, opts:[`Avoid the topic at public meetings until the line has opened`,`Let the mayor's office decide how much to tell the residents`,`Ask the designers to quietly study adding the station back in`,`Meet the association, explain the exclusion, and agree next steps`], a:3, why:`Identify the expectation, explain honestly what's excluded and why, and agree on what the association can expect. Avoidance or a quiet study lets the gap grow until opening day.`, task:`P6`, domain:`People`, approach:`hybrid`, case:`f2c1`}
  ],
  // picks are option indexes as written here: f2s3 is picks[2] (0 leave closed/buriedar, 1 reopen/r_reopened best, 2 quiet look, 3 Aldergrove letter).
  closing:(s,all,G)=>{const re=s.flags.r_reopened||G.r_reopened, bu=s.flags.buriedar||G.buriedar, p=(s.picks||{})[2];
    return [
    `Friday, March 25. Ruth's second page came in the mail, in a long envelope addressed in green ink with a Golden postmark and no return address. Sam didn't open it in the driveway. He set it on the kitchen table against the fruit bowl, made dinner, ate it standing at the counter, and kept an eye on the envelope the whole time, like a dog that might bolt.`,
    (re?`AR 2207 had been open for six weeks. Hana's team had pulled every stand record from the summer of 2059, and Wendell had found the ground bus drawings in a flat file nobody had opened since the fault was fixed. Nothing they'd found yet said noise, and nothing said valve. Hana had started looking at him in meetings again. Not every time.`
      :bu?`AR 2207 was still closed. It was in an archive folder on his drive, where he'd put it, and twice this week he'd caught himself not opening the folder, and noticed the catching, the way you notice a tooth you've been chewing around.`
      :p===2?`Wendell's quiet look had turned into forty pages of printouts in Sam's desk drawer. Nothing in them said noise and nothing said valve, and nobody but the two of them knew they existed, which was starting to feel less like care and more like a second folder named DO NOT CLOSE.`
      :`Aldergrove's letter was attached to AR 2207 now. It said what it said. Sam had read it four times, and each time its last line, signed by an account lead in Pueblo he hadn't met yet, sounded less like a certification and more like a warning.`),
    `At eight he sat down with the tablet against the fruit bowl to answer the last thing in Ada's log. He'd answered the tomatoes weeks ago. He'd left the valve for last.`,
    (re?`<span class="said" data-who="Sam">"There's an old report on a valve. It's real. I reopened it. That's all I can tell you about it right now, and it's true, and when there's more I'll tell you more."</span> He didn't do the voice. He watched it back once to make sure.`
      :bu?`<span class="said" data-who="Sam" data-tone="Smooth, polished, guarded.">"There was an old report on a valve. It's closed."</span> Every word true. He watched it back and heard it, the voice, coming out of his own face. He recorded it again, and it came out the same, and he sent the second one.`
      :p===2?`<span class="said" data-who="Sam" data-tone="Short, guarded, flat.">"Somebody's looking at it."</span> True, and short, and it sounded like a man who'd decided what not to say. He sent it anyway.`
      :`<span class="said" data-who="Sam" data-tone="Smooth, confident, selling.">"There's an old report on a valve. The supplier has certified it."</span> He heard himself say certified the way Garrett said it, and sent it before he could hear it again.`),
    `<span class="said" data-who="Sam">"I haven't met the valve lady. If she's real, I'll find her."</span> That part he meant all the way down.`,
    (s.score>=27
      ?`It had been a good seven weeks, as these things went. He didn't trust the thought. It had the same shape as a thought he'd had once in Arizona, on a Thursday, with a pen in his hand.`
      :`It had been a long seven weeks, and he could name the decisions he'd take back if anybody offered, which nobody was going to.`),
    `He opened Ruth's envelope at 9:40. Page two was inside, the one about the timeline on the wall. Behind it was a photocopy folded into eighths and soft at the creases. He unfolded it on the table, pushing the fruit bowl back to make room.`,
    `It was the timeline. Eleven sheets shrunk onto one, so the bars were thin as thread. The red circles where the bars touched had gone gray in the copy. At the right edge, two vertical lines: the gate. Across the top, in Ruth's 2034 capitals, THE GATE IS REAL. THE BARS IN FRONT OF IT ARE GUESSES.`,
    `He found the propulsion row with his finger. Near the left, early in the flow, a short bar labeled ISOL VLV ACCEPT. In the margin beside it, in green ink, somebody had written a part number.`,
    `<prop type="doc" title="PETREL INTEGRATED TIMELINE · MAY 2034 · PROPULSION ROW (COPY)">ISOL VLV ACCEPT<br>MARGIN, GREEN INK: 7740-112</prop>`,
    `He didn't need to check it. He checked it anyway. The printout of AR 2207 from that first night was in the drawer by the phone charger, under Grace's reading glasses, where he'd put it the night he brought it home from his desk in February, without exactly deciding to. He unfolded it next to the timeline. <b>ALDERGROVE FLUID SYSTEMS P/N 7740-112, REV F.</b>`,
    `Same supplier. Same number. Two ships, twenty five years apart.`,
    `The rest of the copy had faded the way photocopies of old pages fade, everything a little gray. The part number hadn't. It was the exact green of the pen in Ruth's cardigan pocket, and it sat on top of the gray the way new ink does.`,
    `He sat with the two pages side by side until the furnace came on and went off again. Then he got the gas bill out from under the fruit bowl, turned it over, and under minus nine he wrote 7740-112. He didn't know yet what it meant. He knew who did.`
  ];},
  episode:{src:`audio/v2/full-ch02.mp3`, len:`about 60 minutes`},
  next:`The Critical Path`
});
